<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Clinical oral hydration</p>
    <h2>Hydration becomes clinical when context changes what is safe or effective</h2>
    <p>For a healthy person after ordinary activity, choosing a drink can be simple. In healthcare, the same decision may depend on swallowing safety, ongoing losses, sodium or potassium restrictions, kidney or heart function, glucose management, medications, level of consciousness and the reason a person is not drinking normally.</p>
    <p>That is why Best Hydrate's clinical positioning must be evidence-led. A plausible physiology story is not the same as a validated indication, and a consumer hydration product is not automatically interchangeable with a therapeutic oral rehydration solution.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__split">
  <div class="bhd-page__split-media"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/7108157/pexels-photo-7108157.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Doctor discussing care with a patient in a clinical setting" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>
  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">The clinical question</p>
    <h2>What problem are we actually trying to solve?</h2>
    <p>Hydration concern can mean inadequate routine intake, exercise-associated fluid loss, acute gastrointestinal loss, fever, post-procedure intake problems, age-related drinking barriers or a medically complex fluid imbalance. These are not one diagnosis.</p>
    <p>When dehydration is severe, oral intake is unsafe, losses are ongoing or the patient's condition is unstable, assessment and treatment may require a different route or a defined therapeutic formulation.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Clinical decision points</p>
  <h2>Four questions before recommending an oral hydration product</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Can the person swallow safely?</h3><p>Dysphagia, reduced consciousness or aspiration risk can make ordinary thin liquids inappropriate without a swallowing plan.</p></article>
    <article><h3>What is being lost?</h3><p>Low intake, sweat, diarrhoea, vomiting and diuretic use can create different fluid and electrolyte patterns.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Are there restrictions?</h3><p>Heart, kidney, liver and endocrine conditions can change safe fluid, sodium, potassium or carbohydrate intake.</p></article>
    <article><h3>What evidence applies?</h3><p>Product-specific evidence should match the formulation, population, dose, comparator and outcome being claimed.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Best Hydrate and therapeutic ORS</p>
    <h2>Keep overlapping ingredients from becoming a false equivalence</h2>
    <p>WHO reduced-osmolarity ORS is a defined glucose-electrolyte formulation used to prevent or treat dehydration from diarrhoeal disease. Its clinical role comes from its specific composition, intended use and evidence.</p>
    <p>The current Best Hydrate consumer product shares some ingredient classes but should not be described as WHO therapeutic ORS unless the formulation, authorization and evidence support that exact claim. Clinical research can investigate new applications without pretending those applications are already established.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Do not conflate</span><h3>Consumer electrolyte drink ≠ therapeutic ORS.</h3><p>Clinical equivalence requires more than sodium, glucose and overlapping salts on an ingredient list.</p></aside>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Where research can go</p>
  <h2>Clinical hydration questions worth testing properly</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>Adherence and acceptability</h3><p>Will a target population prepare and drink the product consistently, and is it tolerated?</p></article>
    <article><h3>Hydration outcomes</h3><p>Do validated hydration markers improve compared with an appropriate comparator under a defined protocol?</p></article>
    <article><h3>Implementation</h3><p>Can preparation, storage, staffing, procurement and follow-up fit the actual care environment?</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

